WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.080
So, welcome everyone to the lecture today.

00:03.080 --> 00:08.540
We want to continue looking into planning and look into one special form of planning,

00:08.540 --> 00:10.780
namely, planning under uncertainty.

00:10.780 --> 00:16.300
For that, we will investigate the Markov decision processes or in short, MDPs.

00:16.300 --> 00:20.600
So, this is relevant for you whenever you want to plan actions for

00:20.600 --> 00:25.180
an autonomous robot or autonomous system that should act on its own,

00:25.180 --> 00:27.840
but cannot be sure that the action that it

00:27.840 --> 00:30.860
selects are actually executed in the way it wants to be.

00:30.860 --> 00:34.680
So, whenever you have a system which says,

00:34.680 --> 00:37.120
let's say, go one meter per second forward,

00:37.120 --> 00:39.300
and it may not drive one meter per second forward,

00:39.300 --> 00:41.160
it may drive meter per second forward,

00:41.160 --> 00:44.120
but also steers a little bit to the right or to the left-hand side.

00:44.120 --> 00:45.680
If this can happen,

00:45.680 --> 00:52.860
then you need to take MDPs into account in order to plan how to plan under uncertainty,

00:52.860 --> 00:54.940
and that's kind of what MDPs are about.

00:54.940 --> 00:59.420
So, whenever you cannot guarantee that your actions are executed in the way you planned,

00:59.420 --> 01:03.180
you should actually not be using a standard planning algorithm such as A*,

01:03.180 --> 01:05.920
then you should actually use an MDP.

01:05.920 --> 01:08.200
The problem is that MDPs can be computationally

01:08.200 --> 01:12.420
costly or computationally more costly than, for example, the A*, algorithm.

01:12.420 --> 01:16.560
Therefore, a lot of planning systems out there today use A*,

01:16.560 --> 01:20.920
but what you actually would need to do is something like an MDP.

01:20.920 --> 01:25.760
So, again, MDP assumes uncertain motion execution,

01:25.760 --> 01:30.480
but we still assume that we can correctly sense the state of the world.

01:30.480 --> 01:34.400
So, we assume our knowledge about the world is correct,

01:34.400 --> 01:36.200
but we don't know,

01:36.200 --> 01:39.760
we can't guarantee that we execute everything in the way we want it to be.

01:39.760 --> 01:41.120
If both gets uncertain,

01:41.120 --> 01:45.480
then we would have a partially observable Markov decision process or POMDP,

01:45.480 --> 01:49.240
but that's something that we're not going to discuss here today.

01:49.240 --> 01:54.480
So, typically challenges in robot navigation are we need to calculate the optimal paths.

01:54.480 --> 01:57.000
The question, what does optimal mean?

01:57.000 --> 02:00.600
So, we have some cost function and we're going to come up with the optimal paths.

02:00.600 --> 02:04.840
First, can we actually guarantee that we find the optimal paths?

02:04.840 --> 02:06.080
The second thing is,

02:06.080 --> 02:09.920
is actually our notation of optimality actually what we want to have?

02:09.920 --> 02:16.040
So, if the underlying cost function actually leading to the behavior that we want the system to have.

02:16.040 --> 02:18.400
We have seen this already with the A*, algorithm.

02:18.400 --> 02:23.120
If, let's say, the shortest path is our criterion for optimality,

02:23.120 --> 02:27.960
it will actually lead to navigation behaviors where the robot goes really, really, really close to obstacles.

02:27.960 --> 02:33.360
And this can lead to a collision if something is not exactly the way we perceived it to be,

02:33.360 --> 02:37.920
or the system doesn't exactly execute what it's supposed to do.

02:37.920 --> 02:41.080
And therefore, this may not be actually the optimal path,

02:41.080 --> 02:45.640
although you're typically looking for navigating along the shortest paths.

02:45.680 --> 02:49.040
Other challenges are how to deal with unforeseen objects.

02:49.040 --> 02:52.160
So, if new objects pop up in the scene that the robot is not aware of,

02:52.160 --> 02:55.160
that's not in the robot's map, how to react to this?

02:55.160 --> 02:58.280
What should we even do if objects are moving around?

02:58.280 --> 03:02.320
How can we take the movement into account to predict how those objects behave

03:02.320 --> 03:05.920
and take those predictions into account in planning?

03:05.920 --> 03:13.440
Or, last but not least, how should we behave if we don't know if the system actually does what we want it to do?

03:13.440 --> 03:15.920
And that's what we are looking in here today,

03:15.920 --> 03:22.400
the MDP, Markov Decision Process, taking into account uncertainties in the motion execution.

03:22.400 --> 03:28.120
So that's the task that we are going to investigate here today.

03:28.120 --> 03:32.480
If you look to our layered architecture that we have discussed in the context of A*,

03:32.480 --> 03:38.080
we typically have a planning level where the planner runs at rather low frequency,

03:38.080 --> 03:42.120
let's say once every second or once every two seconds, for example.

03:42.120 --> 03:46.680
Then, we have typically a local planner or local collision avoidance system depending.

03:46.680 --> 03:49.560
So, this can be a local planner, it can be a collision avoidance system,

03:49.560 --> 03:51.360
the boundaries are rather fluent here,

03:51.360 --> 03:54.800
which runs at the high frequency, takes sensor data into account,

03:54.800 --> 04:03.480
and generates trajectories, either guide the robot on the shortest trajectory that comes from the planner here on top,

04:03.480 --> 04:11.280
or generates, let's say, swerves or other behaviors in order to kind of avoid an obstacle that is in front of the system.

04:11.280 --> 04:19.000
And then, on the lowest level, we have a controller that makes sure or tries to make sure that what we're actually sending to the system is actually being executed.

04:19.000 --> 04:25.520
And this cannot be guaranteed because of timing issues in terms of kinematic constraints,

04:25.520 --> 04:29.640
in the sense that we do not know all the, let's say, surface properties we are driving on,

04:29.640 --> 04:32.360
or not all properties of the robot itself.

04:32.360 --> 04:34.520
So, this will not be perfect.

04:34.520 --> 04:39.400
And today, we are dealing with the fact that this is actually not perfect.

04:39.400 --> 04:44.560
We do this on the planning level, that means we do this here in this planning level layer,

04:44.560 --> 04:51.240
that already the planner takes into account that whatever it tells the system down here may not be executed perfectly.

04:51.240 --> 04:55.440
So, let's take into account that what we do here is imperfect,

04:55.440 --> 05:01.720
and maybe generate trajectories, for example, that guide the system away from obstacles a little bit further.

05:01.720 --> 05:07.360
So, even if we take some uncertainty in the motion execution into account, this will not lead to collisions.

05:07.360 --> 05:11.040
And that's basically what the MDP system does.

05:11.040 --> 05:13.560
So, let's go with a very simplistic example.

05:13.560 --> 05:17.520
So, these are these typical small-scale AI examples.

05:17.520 --> 05:22.960
You have an agent or your robot that is acting in an environment, and we have here a starting location,

05:22.960 --> 05:27.320
these are all free cells, here's an obstacle, and here is a goal state,

05:27.320 --> 05:30.040
and here is a state where the system doesn't want to end up.

05:30.040 --> 05:35.480
So, the plus one is the state we want to be, the minus one is the state which we don't want, never want to reach,

05:35.480 --> 05:42.200
and this is where we start, and the question is, how do I actually reach this location down here?

05:42.200 --> 05:46.600
So, for a robot, this could be, this is a charging station where the robot can recharge itself,

05:46.600 --> 05:51.680
and this is a staircase, and if the robot falls down the staircase, the robot will be broken or will be dead.

05:51.680 --> 05:56.480
So, let's state that the system doesn't want to end up in.

05:56.480 --> 06:00.360
And so, the question is, how should the system now navigate in this world?

06:00.360 --> 06:05.840
Assume it can always go whatever, up, down, right or left, or north, south, east, west,

06:05.840 --> 06:09.040
and so the system can always navigate here through those cells,

06:09.040 --> 06:13.320
and it wants to go here, but it never wants to reach that state over here.

06:13.320 --> 06:19.960
So, if we do this in our traditional A-star planning world, then this is an easy problem.

06:19.960 --> 06:23.160
We can compute the shortest path in a very easy way,

06:23.160 --> 06:29.880
and this shortest path would guide the system here to the side, and then going up, up, and again to the right,

06:29.880 --> 06:38.200
so that the system ends up in the location where the charging station is, and can recharge its batteries, right?

06:38.200 --> 06:42.760
So, that's easy, that's something that we can do with A-star.

06:42.760 --> 06:44.880
So, the question is, why might this be problematic?

06:44.880 --> 06:53.720
Why this, may this be the shortest path, but not necessarily the best path that the system may execute?

06:53.720 --> 06:57.560
The reason for this, why this may be problematic, or is typically problematic in reality,

06:57.560 --> 07:01.880
is that actions are not executed with probability one.

07:01.880 --> 07:06.040
That means we have an imperfect or non-perfect action execution,

07:06.040 --> 07:11.720
so actions will only be executed in the right way with a certain probability, let's say 90%.

07:11.720 --> 07:15.240
90% of the cases the system exactly executes what I want to execute,

07:15.240 --> 07:17.720
and in 10% of the cases it does something else.

07:17.720 --> 07:22.200
This would be an example for such a transition model.

07:22.200 --> 07:27.480
And the question is then, given that we acknowledge the fact that our system is not perfect,

07:27.480 --> 07:32.600
what can we do on a planning level to take this imperfection into account?

07:32.600 --> 07:36.840
So, is there a way that we take into account that not everything we do is great,

07:36.840 --> 07:39.480
but in the end make better decisions?

07:39.480 --> 07:44.680
So, this is for example a mobile robot that doesn't always perform exactly the desired motion commands,

07:44.680 --> 07:50.600
so how can we actually realize a planning system which takes this into account?

07:50.600 --> 07:52.120
But we can also think it further.

07:52.120 --> 07:57.080
If you think of your sat-nav device that you are using in your car in order to navigate,

07:57.080 --> 08:00.600
it gives you a command and you as a human, you are the driver,

08:00.600 --> 08:05.400
and if the system says turn right on the next intersection and you're not turning right,

08:05.400 --> 08:08.040
then this is an imperfect motion execution,

08:08.040 --> 08:12.120
because you're not executing what the planning system tells you.

08:12.120 --> 08:14.600
So, the question would be from the sat-nav point of view,

08:14.600 --> 08:19.240
can the sat-nav do better motion instructions or road instructions for you,

08:19.960 --> 08:25.880
so that if you make errors and don't always execute what the system tells you to execute,

08:25.880 --> 08:29.640
so you still come up to the goal location on a good path?

08:29.640 --> 08:31.160
This is another example,

08:31.160 --> 08:37.480
and so it's a general decision-making setup for how to make decisions under uncertainty

08:37.480 --> 08:39.400
in your action execution,

08:39.400 --> 08:41.320
and this is relevant not only in robotics,

08:41.320 --> 08:44.200
it's also relevant in a lot of other disciplines and other fields,

08:44.200 --> 08:49.320
where you cannot guarantee that what you decide will actually be done in that way.

08:49.320 --> 08:51.880
So, whenever you do this decision-making under uncertainty,

08:51.880 --> 08:57.880
the lecture today will tell you on how to behave in an optimal way.

08:58.840 --> 09:03.480
Okay, let's start with a very simple example similar to what I've shown before.

09:03.480 --> 09:07.400
So, you are still in our simulated agent world,

09:07.400 --> 09:09.720
and the agent can select certain commands,

09:09.720 --> 09:12.200
but it doesn't execute those commands perfectly.

09:12.200 --> 09:15.240
So, let's say if the desired action is to go north or to go up,

09:16.040 --> 09:19.080
with 80% of the cases it actually executes this,

09:19.080 --> 09:21.880
and with 10% of the case it goes either left or right.

09:22.600 --> 09:28.120
So, 80% probability up, 10% to the left, and 10% to the right.

09:28.120 --> 09:29.640
So, assuming that this is our model,

09:29.640 --> 09:32.840
so in 80% of the cases exactly happens what we predict,

09:32.840 --> 09:35.480
in 10% of the cases I'm either turning left or right,

09:35.480 --> 09:37.160
and not go straight as I want it to be.

09:38.360 --> 09:43.400
So, this means again the intended action is executed with 80% probability,

09:43.400 --> 09:47.640
and with 10% probability I'm moving left, and with 10% probability I'm moving right,

09:47.640 --> 09:51.320
and let's say I assume if I drive into a wall I'm just bumped back from the wall.

09:51.320 --> 09:54.520
So, we don't get any penalty for bumping into a wall right now.

09:55.080 --> 09:55.880
Let's assume this.

09:56.440 --> 09:59.080
Okay, so we again have our A-star plan.

09:59.080 --> 10:04.200
That was a plan that A-star planned assuming perfect action execution.

10:04.760 --> 10:10.760
So, it will turn go right, go right, go up, go up, go right.

10:10.760 --> 10:12.520
So, this is what the system would do.

10:13.800 --> 10:16.200
Consider now that my action execution is not perfect.

10:16.200 --> 10:19.320
So, with a probability of 10% in each state,

10:19.320 --> 10:24.840
I would go 10% here, 10% here, 10% here, 10% here, 10% here, 10% here.

10:24.840 --> 10:29.560
So, this is my non-deterministic motion model that I'm taking into account.

10:29.560 --> 10:32.280
And the question, what happens if I do this?

10:33.080 --> 10:38.600
The problem is at some point in time you will actually end up here,

10:38.600 --> 10:40.520
because the system will go right, it will go up,

10:41.080 --> 10:43.320
when you give the go up command it will actually turn right,

10:43.880 --> 10:47.320
and then the robot will fall down the staircase and the robot will be dead.

10:47.320 --> 10:50.200
And that's a state that the robot doesn't want to be in.

10:50.200 --> 10:53.480
So, what can we do in order to prevent that situation?

10:54.280 --> 10:57.560
What we can do is we can choose another trajectory,

10:57.560 --> 11:01.960
which is longer and not optimal under the shortest path criterion,

11:02.600 --> 11:08.680
but which will reduce the probability that we actually end up on the staircase.

11:09.240 --> 11:13.160
So, we want to reduce the probability of not reaching the goal.

11:13.320 --> 11:16.680
How could we behave in order to end up here at that goal?

11:16.680 --> 11:22.680
So, we could not go right because it drives us close to the staircase.

11:22.680 --> 11:26.360
So, we take this detour around this obstacle because then we're on a trajectory

11:26.360 --> 11:30.280
which is less likely to fall down the staircase

11:30.280 --> 11:32.360
because we're keeping a distance to the staircase.

11:33.080 --> 11:35.640
And this can be seen as a more conservative strategy,

11:36.280 --> 11:42.360
a strategy which avoids the possibility of falling down the staircase and dying

11:42.360 --> 11:46.520
by paying a little bit more in terms of path lengths,

11:46.520 --> 11:51.640
but then increasing the probability of actually reaching my target location.

11:51.640 --> 11:53.880
And it's kind of the motivation for the MDPs,

11:53.880 --> 11:56.920
and this is actually the solution that an MDP can generate

11:57.800 --> 12:01.560
in order to make sure the system doesn't fall down the staircase

12:01.560 --> 12:05.160
but ends up at the charging station.

12:05.240 --> 12:13.800
Okay, so what obviously plays an important role in this is this so-called transition model.

12:13.800 --> 12:18.760
So, this definition of how the system executes the motion,

12:18.760 --> 12:22.920
what I said, 80%, we execute the motion, 10%, left 10%, right,

12:23.800 --> 12:25.400
of the case we do something else.

12:25.400 --> 12:29.800
And this can be more formally described by a probability distribution

12:29.800 --> 12:34.920
to reach the next state s prime given that we are in s and execute this action a.

12:35.720 --> 12:42.840
And if you think about the localization problem that we have investigated in this course before,

12:42.840 --> 12:46.600
then you see this is actually very similar to our probabilistic motion model,

12:46.600 --> 12:50.200
p of x t given x t minus 1 u t.

12:51.560 --> 12:57.800
x corresponds here to s, the state, and a corresponds here to the odometry u t,

12:57.800 --> 12:59.160
or the control u t.

12:59.160 --> 13:01.960
So this is nothing else than the probabilistic motion model

13:01.960 --> 13:04.200
that we have been discussing before.

13:04.200 --> 13:09.960
What the probability of ending up at s prime given we are in s and execute action a.

13:10.680 --> 13:15.160
The somehow difference in here is what we do here is typically happens in a discretized world,

13:15.160 --> 13:16.840
in a discretized state space,

13:16.840 --> 13:20.760
and before we often have a non-discretized state space,

13:20.760 --> 13:23.800
for example in the context of the Kalman filter or the particle filter,

13:23.800 --> 13:28.360
but it's kind of the same thing just living in a discretized world over here.

13:28.360 --> 13:31.880
And this is called a transition model in the notation of an MDP.

13:32.760 --> 13:37.400
And this notation, what we assume that this transition model has a Markov property,

13:37.400 --> 13:43.240
that's also something that we have assumed in probabilistic localization,

13:43.240 --> 13:49.560
and that is that the probability of ending up in s prime from s and a

13:49.560 --> 13:52.520
doesn't depend on states we have been in before s.

13:52.520 --> 13:57.240
So if we know where we are in at a certain point in time, the past doesn't matter anymore.

13:57.240 --> 14:02.840
It's basically knowing the current state renders the past independent from the future.

14:02.840 --> 14:07.560
And this is exactly what we are exploiting or a property that we're exploiting here,

14:07.560 --> 14:10.600
that the transition model only depends on the current state I'm in,

14:10.600 --> 14:12.440
and on the command I'm executing,

14:12.440 --> 14:15.560
and this tells me where I'm going to end up with,

14:15.560 --> 14:18.600
but the history, the past doesn't matter anymore.

14:19.880 --> 14:22.920
So this is what our constraints about the transition model.

14:23.560 --> 14:25.880
Now the question is, given we have that transition model,

14:25.880 --> 14:29.320
how do we specify that something is good or something is bad?

14:29.320 --> 14:34.120
So that reaching the charging station is good while reaching the staircase is bad.

14:34.120 --> 14:37.720
How can we actually encode this? How can we formulate this?

14:37.720 --> 14:42.920
And this is something that we are doing taking the so-called reward function into account.

14:42.920 --> 14:47.880
A reward is something that the agent gets if he reaches a certain state.

14:47.880 --> 14:49.240
So see this as a number.

14:49.800 --> 14:53.000
Let's say a positive number is good, a negative number is bad,

14:53.000 --> 14:55.480
and whenever you do something good you get a positive reward.

14:55.480 --> 14:57.640
Whenever you do something bad you get a negative reward.

14:58.920 --> 15:03.320
We are not defining this here necessarily in the general form,

15:03.320 --> 15:07.480
where in a state doing something leads you to a new state gives you a reward.

15:07.480 --> 15:10.600
We just boil down the reward that you get the reward in a certain state.

15:10.600 --> 15:13.480
So if you reach a certain state you get a positive or negative reward.

15:13.480 --> 15:16.040
But this doesn't change the overall problem,

15:16.760 --> 15:19.560
but for now we kind of keep this slightly more simple

15:19.560 --> 15:21.640
by saying the reward only depends on the state.

15:21.640 --> 15:25.800
So depending the state you're in you're getting a reward either good or bad.

15:25.800 --> 15:29.480
So if you, let's say, go to the staircase you will get a very big negative reward

15:29.480 --> 15:31.640
because it was bad you get punished for it.

15:31.640 --> 15:36.280
If you reach the charging station that was good you get a positive reward for that.

15:36.280 --> 15:38.520
Okay, so this is the reward function.

15:38.520 --> 15:42.120
The only constraint that we have that the reward function must be bounded.

15:42.120 --> 15:46.440
So it can't be infinitely large but it can take positive or negative numbers

15:46.440 --> 15:50.520
or it can also take the value of zero which basically means it doesn't change anything.

15:51.720 --> 15:57.080
Okay, let's have a look how we could actually specify the reward function in our example.

15:57.080 --> 16:00.040
So assume we have our simplified world over here with our agent

16:00.680 --> 16:03.800
and what we need to do we need to specify a reward for every state.

16:03.800 --> 16:07.800
So every state gets a value and if the system is in that state,

16:07.800 --> 16:09.880
the agent is in that state, it gets this reward.

16:10.440 --> 16:13.000
Okay, we said the charging station is plus one, that's good.

16:13.000 --> 16:15.880
The staircase is minus one, that's bad.

16:16.760 --> 16:20.600
But for all other states we're giving the system a small negative reward.

16:21.320 --> 16:23.720
Right, what does the small negative reward mean?

16:23.720 --> 16:27.800
That means if the system stays in the state it gets punished.

16:27.800 --> 16:30.760
So it doesn't get as much punished as falling down the staircase

16:30.760 --> 16:33.720
but you get kind of hit with a small whip, let's say it that way.

16:33.720 --> 16:37.960
So you can also formulate this like living in this environment is not enjoyable.

16:37.960 --> 16:39.880
The world sucks so to say.

16:39.880 --> 16:43.320
And you basically get punished for every second you're in that world.

16:43.320 --> 16:44.920
For every time you're in the state,

16:45.640 --> 16:48.760
if you think of you can let's say visit one state per second,

16:48.760 --> 16:52.200
then for every second you spent in this environment you get punished.

16:53.000 --> 16:57.240
And you do that in order to make the system achieve something.

16:58.120 --> 17:00.920
If you would keep the reward here at zero,

17:01.640 --> 17:03.720
the system would say, I don't need to do anything.

17:03.720 --> 17:06.600
Nothing will happen to me, nothing good, but also nothing bad.

17:06.600 --> 17:09.080
I don't age, I don't care about anything.

17:09.080 --> 17:10.840
This would be the zero reward.

17:10.840 --> 17:12.440
But this is something that we don't want to have.

17:12.440 --> 17:15.240
We don't want to generate a behavior where the system doesn't care

17:15.240 --> 17:17.240
on what it should do, it would just stay where it is.

17:17.720 --> 17:22.840
We want to, which takes infinite amount of time to maybe go to the goal,

17:22.840 --> 17:26.840
could circle around this obstacle forever until it reaches the state

17:26.840 --> 17:28.920
or always jumps forth and back between two states

17:28.920 --> 17:30.520
because it doesn't get punished for that.

17:31.160 --> 17:33.240
So we want to make it unpleasant for the robot.

17:33.240 --> 17:38.360
We want to give the robot, put it into a non enjoyable space

17:38.360 --> 17:40.760
because then the system wants to leave this space

17:40.760 --> 17:43.160
and get an incentive for moving away.

17:43.160 --> 17:47.000
And we achieve this by giving a very small negative reward.

17:47.480 --> 17:50.040
Your life sucks as long as you're in this world,

17:50.040 --> 17:51.800
but it will be great if you reach the goal.

17:51.800 --> 17:54.200
So you can formulate it like this.

17:54.200 --> 17:56.280
So this can be our reward function.

17:56.280 --> 18:00.120
And then with this reward function, we can actually make the system act.

18:00.120 --> 18:03.080
And depending on the ratio between those rewards,

18:03.960 --> 18:06.120
the system will behave differently.

18:07.320 --> 18:09.720
And now the question is, how can we define all that?

18:09.720 --> 18:14.120
How can we formally define an MDP or Markov decision process?

18:14.680 --> 18:16.760
So given a sequential decision problem,

18:16.760 --> 18:19.560
so a problem where we make one decision after each other,

18:19.560 --> 18:21.240
which is fully observable,

