36:11.360 --> 36:24.960
So it's R of S plus the maximum of the future utilities, and this is exactly the sum over how likely it is that I'm ending up in a state times the utility of that future state.

36:25.960 --> 36:45.360
So what happened, I have broken up the utility of a state, so the true utility of a state, into the reward of that state plus the expectation over the reward of the successor states, which can be computed by the sum over all states I'm ending up as a next step.

36:45.840 --> 36:51.240
The transition probability of ending up there times the true utility of that successor state.

36:51.360 --> 36:53.160
This is exactly what happens in here.

36:54.160 --> 37:02.120
And this equation here is something which is very famously known as the Balman equation, which provides me the true utility of a state.

37:03.120 --> 37:25.960
This work was done by Balman in the end of the 15th, 1957, I think, when he published a book, or he's one of the masters of dynamic programming, the book is actually called Dynamic Programming, where he provides this function for computing, for optimal decision making with this reward function and these utilities.

37:25.960 --> 37:39.960
And it assumes again that you can break up these steps in a certain way so that you kind of have a separable problem, but under this assumption it tells you the optimal utility of the state that you can then use for decision making.

37:39.960 --> 37:53.960
And what it shows is that there's a direct relationship between the state itself, this R S, and where I'm going to up, so its neighbours, its neighbouring states, those states which I can reach through carrying out a certain action.

37:53.960 --> 38:05.960
So the utility of a state is the immediate reward, so the kind of short-term reward that I get, and the long-term utility, the expected utility of the next state, given that I'm behaving in an optimal way.

38:05.960 --> 38:12.960
And we can compute this U S for all states. So for all states we can compute this utility.

38:13.960 --> 38:18.960
The question is how do we actually, how can we actually do this? How can we obtain those computations?

38:18.960 --> 38:26.960
Okay, let's have a look. So we have N states, so N possible states that are in, and N unknowns in terms of N of those rewards.

38:26.960 --> 38:35.960
So can we use just kind of our tools that we know from linear algebra in order to solve this system with N states and N unknowns?

38:35.960 --> 38:40.960
Unfortunately this is not the case and one of the reasons is this maximisation operator.

38:40.960 --> 38:53.960
It's a non-linear operator and it's an operator which is hard to optimise for and it clearly needs, it's a non-linear equation and therefore we go for an iterative approach.

38:53.960 --> 39:01.960
So we start with initial configuration and then update that and iterate until we converge into the right state.

39:01.960 --> 39:16.960
And this is something that Bellman has shown in this book, it's a classical dynamic programming example that we can use this iterative approach in order to compute the optimal utility for any state.

39:16.960 --> 39:23.960
So while doing this we have made an assumption on the way. This is the assumption of separability.

39:23.960 --> 39:37.960
This basically means that we cannot use all utility functions in here, they have to have a certain property and this is basically that I can actually break up this sequence of actions into a reward plus the utility of the remaining actions.

39:37.960 --> 39:47.960
So only if I can actually make the separation that if I'm in the current state as zero I can separate this state out and compute the reward plus the utility of the remaining state.

39:47.960 --> 39:55.960
So they kind of break this down in sequence. That means if I'm in a certain state where I have been before basically it doesn't matter anymore.

39:55.960 --> 40:03.960
So this decision making only holds for problems that have this kind of property.

40:03.960 --> 40:10.960
Another thing we typically do is we want to kind of discount our utility or our rewards over time.

40:10.960 --> 40:19.960
So in the computation of the utility of a policy we don't sum up the rewards as they are, we have a discount factor here for the reward.

40:19.960 --> 40:25.960
And the discount factor which says rewards that I'm getting later on the future count less than rewards that I'm getting right now.

40:25.960 --> 40:29.960
So it's more what I get right now is better than what I'm getting in the future.

40:29.960 --> 40:35.960
So if I get let's say 100 euros today is better than getting 100 euros in a year.

40:35.960 --> 40:39.960
So we are discounting the reward of this 100 euro over time.

40:39.960 --> 40:54.960
And we can do this here in order to say we want to optimize for a system which gets where the immediate rewards is more important than the rewards I'm getting in some far future.

40:54.960 --> 40:58.960
Of course if in some far future the reward is much bigger I'm still going for it.

40:58.960 --> 41:01.960
But I'm taking this discounting into account.

41:01.960 --> 41:06.960
So I have basically a discount factor in here that kind of reduces the reward over time.

41:06.960 --> 41:20.960
This is something that is typically done so that we don't optimize or don't spend too much time in states if we have around zero reward and kind of discount the reward the longer it takes me to actually achieve my goal.

41:20.960 --> 41:27.960
You can see this the more states I need to visit the longer it takes and I actually want to minimize this and do this as quickly as possible.

41:28.960 --> 41:37.960
Okay, if we take our example that we had before and compute the utility of the state, so the true utility, these will be the true utilities that we actually get.

41:37.960 --> 41:50.960
So the different states have different utilities and they have a high utility if they are close to the goal because if I'm in that state I can reach my goal location closer.

41:50.960 --> 42:01.960
It's better but if I'm for example close to a bad state I'm getting a substantially smaller reward but given that it's also close to the goal it's somewhere in between.

42:01.960 --> 42:11.960
But if you're kind of close to the goal a little bit further away from the negative reward from the bad state your utility will be substantially higher.

42:11.960 --> 42:17.960
And the question is how do I do this computation? How do I end up there when I reach this state?

42:17.960 --> 42:20.960
So what we do, what we said, we have this iterative computation.

42:20.960 --> 42:29.960
So we update our utility of a state by taking the reward of that state into account and the utility of the neighboring states.

42:29.960 --> 42:42.960
And we start basically with, we initialize our utilities, zero initialization, initialization doesn't really matter, it doesn't impact how fast the system converges but not on the result.

42:42.960 --> 42:58.960
And the key idea is that for utilities, so this is the iteration, in the new iteration is the reward in that state and then I'm maximizing over the sum of the transitions of the other utilities in the neighboring states at the previous iteration.

42:58.960 --> 43:04.960
So if you start for example with a zero utility, so all states have zero utility.

43:04.960 --> 43:14.960
In the first iteration this will all be zero, so all this term cancels out to zero and I'm just setting the reward as the utility for a state.

43:14.960 --> 43:26.960
And then after this we have the reward as initial state and then this part starts to play a role and you're updating this maximizing the future reward over time.

43:26.960 --> 43:36.960
And this approach will lead to an optimal solution, completing the optimal utility if your time goes towards infinity.

43:36.960 --> 43:48.960
Of course we typically don't have infinite time, we don't want to wait for an infinite amount of time to come up with a solution, so we are basically monitoring the utility and simply stop if the change in the utility is smaller than the threshold.

43:48.960 --> 44:02.960
So for example if the root mean square error here between the difference between subsequent states for all states is below a certain threshold, I'm actually stopping my operation or another criterion that I may use.

44:02.960 --> 44:12.960
And this algorithm which is sketched here is something which is famously known as the value iteration algorithm, which is the key algorithm in dynamic programming to solve the MDPs.

44:12.960 --> 44:24.960
And again we have the definition of all states and the discount factor, I'm starting setting all utilities to zero and then I'm basically iterating with this equation that we have shown over here.

44:24.960 --> 44:42.960
So updating the utility as a reward of the state plus the discount factor of the utility of the neighboring states and the time of the probability of ending up there, I'm maximizing for this and I'm repeating this until u and u prime are close enough.

44:42.960 --> 44:56.960
So basically when the utility between two iterations is close enough, and this close enough could be some distance function between the utilities, if the change is smaller than epsilon then you're actually aborting.

44:56.960 --> 45:06.960
And this is how value iteration is run. You can also make a small example, let's say we have a very simplified setup just for showing this here as the purpose of illustration.

45:07.960 --> 45:22.960
We use our same transition model as before, so with 80% we do what we want to do with 10% left right each and let's say this is the states that I have and this gives me the reward function, so reward 1, reward 10, reward 5, reward 1, reward minus 8.

45:22.960 --> 45:31.960
And the question is what is now the utility of this state in the center over here.

45:32.960 --> 45:40.960
So what I'm doing, I take the reward of that state, the reward is 1, and then I take the maximum of all actions that I can execute.

45:40.960 --> 45:44.960
So should I go left, should I go right, should I go up, should I go down?

45:44.960 --> 45:55.960
And what basically happens is if I go left, so if I'm in that state and I want to go left, I'm ending up with 10% probability here with 80% probability here and this 10% probability here.

45:55.960 --> 46:11.960
So this would be 0.1 times 1, so 0 times 1 times 1, 0 times 8, 80% probability times 5 is this utility, 0.1 times 10 is this utility, so this is what I would get for moving left.

46:11.960 --> 46:31.960
And do the same for up, right, and down, so that this simplifies to 1 plus the maximum of 5.1, 7.7, minus 5.3, and minus 0.5, so 7.7 would be the best thing, 1 plus 7.7 is 8.7, so this state would get a utility of 8.7.

46:31.960 --> 46:37.960
And then we're doing this for all states, repeating this process over and over and over again until convergence.

46:38.960 --> 46:43.960
And for example, for our simple example, this looks like this, so this is the utility for the different states.

46:43.960 --> 46:51.960
So this is state 1,1, this is 4,3, so 1,2,3,4 for the first number, and 1,2,3 for the second number.

46:51.960 --> 46:58.960
Then you can see how the utility of the state starting being initialized down here evolves over time until the states converge.

46:58.960 --> 47:10.960
So we can see here roughly after, whatever, something around 10 iterations or 12 iterations, the system would have converged here and come up with the optimal, or with the true utility of a state.

47:10.960 --> 47:20.960
And as a result of this, I have my optimal utility of a state, and then can easily from that extract the policy.

47:21.960 --> 47:27.960
So the value iteration finds the optimal solution for this Markov decision process.

47:27.960 --> 47:32.960
It will converge to a unique solution of the Bellman equation system.

47:32.960 --> 47:39.960
The initial values are arbitrary, that means, of course, the closer the values are to the true values, the faster they will converge.

47:39.960 --> 47:44.960
But if I have an infinite number of operations of iterations, I will converge to the solution anyhow.

47:44.960 --> 47:50.960
And what the value iteration does, it basically propagates the information through the state space through these local updates.

47:50.960 --> 47:56.960
And these local updates are given through the transition model and the utilities of the neighboring states.

47:56.960 --> 48:04.960
So with this, I'm getting the optimal utility for every state, so the true utility for every state.

48:04.960 --> 48:08.960
And what I then can do is I can compute the optimal policy directly out of this.

48:08.960 --> 48:19.960
Because if I know the true utility of a state, I just can walk into the neighboring state where I'm actually maximizing my true utility.

48:19.960 --> 48:24.960
And I do this by taking into account that my motion is uncertain.

48:24.960 --> 48:32.960
So if I say what should I do in a certain state, I'm just taking the action which maximizes this sum.

48:32.960 --> 48:44.960
So the probability of ending up of going whatever left in the utility of the state left plus the probability of going up and getting the utility of the state up and the probability of going right and getting the utility right.

48:44.960 --> 48:50.960
But this is then if I have these values over here, I just need to iterate only about overall actions I can execute.

48:50.960 --> 48:53.960
I'm basically computing what the utility of that action is.

48:53.960 --> 48:58.960
I'm taking the one which maximizes this activity.

48:58.960 --> 49:02.960
So I'm basically taking the best action under this function.

49:02.960 --> 49:07.960
And this gives me the optimal policy and thus the optimal behavior out of this.

49:07.960 --> 49:09.960
So a few notes on the utility and the reward.

49:09.960 --> 49:14.960
So it's important to note that reward and utilities are related quantities, but they are different.

49:14.960 --> 49:17.960
The reward R can be seen as a short-term reward.

49:17.960 --> 49:21.960
So what I'm getting if I'm in a state, the reward that the system gets.

49:21.960 --> 49:24.960
It's intermediate direct reward it gets in state S.

49:24.960 --> 49:28.960
The utility however is more something like a long-term reward.

49:28.960 --> 49:30.960
So how good is the state in the long run?

49:30.960 --> 49:34.960
If I'm traveling through this state, will it bring me closer to the goal?

49:34.960 --> 49:40.960
So the difference between what I get right now and an investment into the future.

49:40.960 --> 49:42.960
So short-term reward and long-term reward.

49:42.960 --> 49:45.960
And they need to be distinguished because we are interested in the long-term reward.

49:45.960 --> 49:52.960
If we don't want to wait too long, we take this discounting factor into account which reduces the utility of states where the reward comes towards the end.

49:52.960 --> 49:59.960
But typically I'm interested about the reward that I can accumulate on the way towards my final state.

49:59.960 --> 50:04.960
So what I presented here was a very basic introduction to Markov decision processes.

50:04.960 --> 50:11.960
And this is a technique that is used for decision making under uncertainty.

50:11.960 --> 50:19.960
So whenever you cannot guarantee that the actions you're executing will actually be executed the way you want them to be executed.

50:19.960 --> 50:22.960
Then you should take that into account when selecting your actions.

50:22.960 --> 50:24.960
And the MDP tells you how to do this.

50:24.960 --> 50:30.960
What the MDP assumes is that the world is fully observable, so you know the state of the system at all points in time.

50:30.960 --> 50:36.960
You have this separability property that we have before, our transition model is Markovian.

50:36.960 --> 50:42.960
So it follows the Markov property, something that we anyway assumed in this course nearly in all models.

50:42.960 --> 50:51.960
And then the MDP gives you the optimal solution through the value iteration by computing the utility of a state.

50:51.960 --> 50:55.960
And then once we have the utility, extract the policy out of this.

50:55.960 --> 51:06.960
There are also variants of this such as policy iteration and a large number of other techniques that you can apply in order to speed up the processes, take additional uncertainties into account and so on.

51:06.960 --> 51:12.960
But what I presented here are the basics of a Markov decision process and hope could explain you what they are useful for.

